---
title: "Interactive and explorable documents"
---

Taliesin runs `{js}` cells in the browser and wires them into a small reactive
graph. On top of that graph sit two declarative conveniences, inputs and
scroll-driven scenes, plus two scroll and tab layouts. Everything here is
client-side and offline, built from the same block model as the rest of the page,
and the preview stays read-only: a reader interacts with the output, never the
source.

The examples below are shown as source listings. Each feature has a runnable
counterpart in the corpus (`corpus/reactive/`, `corpus/explorable/`,
`corpus/narrate/`, `corpus/layout/`).

## Reactive `{js}` cells

A `{js}` cell declares its place in a dependency graph with leading `//|`
directives (they must come before any code):

- `//| viewof: NAME` makes the cell an input control published under `NAME`.
- `//| name: NAME` publishes the cell's return value under `NAME`.
- `//| input: A, B` consumes those names; the cell re-runs when any of them change.

Inside a cell, read values through the injected `qmd` API: `qmd.value("k")` returns
a control's current value, and `qmd.get("squared")` returns the last value a
`//| name` cell published. A first cell publishes a slider as `n`:

```js
//| viewof: n
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.value = "3";
return input;
```

A second derives a value from it, and a third consumes that derived value, so the
chain `n` to `squared` to the readout stays in sync transitively:

```js
//| name: squared
//| input: n
return qmd.value("n") ** 2;
```

```js
//| input: squared
return document.createTextNode("n squared = " + qmd.get("squared"));
```

When an input changes, Taliesin re-runs exactly the cells downstream of it, once
each, in dependency order, following `//| name` edges. This is one controlled pass,
not a continuously-recomputing dataflow engine: cycles are detected and shown as an
error rather than run. `Plot` and `d3` are available as globals for drawing; a cell
returns a DOM node (mounted for you) or builds into `qmd.container`, and an
`invalidation` promise lets long-running cells (animations, WebGL) tear down before
a re-run.

## Inputs without boilerplate: `{{< input >}}`

The `{{< input >}}` shortcode emits a labelled, keyboard-accessible control whose
value becomes a reactive node, so a `{js}` cell reacts with `//| input:` and no
control-building code. Five types are available:

```markdown
{{< input name="k" type="slider" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="3" label="k" >}}
{{< input name="n" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="20" label="n" >}}
{{< input name="on" type="checkbox" value="true" label="enabled" >}}
{{< input name="q" type="text" value="hello" label="query" >}}
{{< input name="c" type="select" options="alpha,beta,gamma" value="beta" label="channel" >}}
```

`name` is required (it is the reactive node); `type` defaults to `slider`; `label`
defaults to the name. `qmd.value()` returns a number for slider and number, a
boolean for checkbox, and a string for text and select. A consuming cell is then just
a `{js}` cell with `//| input: k` that reads `qmd.value("k")`.

**`slider` is an alias of `range`.** Both render the same HTML `<input type="range">`
and both get a live `<output>` readout that updates as you drag, so the reader always
sees the current value beside the track. `number` shares their numeric `min`/`max`/
`step`/`value` attributes but renders as a spinner with no readout. The two names are
interchangeable; use whichever reads better in your source.

A **`select`** gives a closed set of choices. Its `options` are a comma-separated list,
and `options` is required for this type (omit it and `check` warns). `value` picks the
initially selected option:

```markdown
{{< input name="palette" type="select" options="viridis,magma,cividis" value="magma" label="Palette" >}}
```

That publishes a reactive node `palette` whose value is the chosen string. A chart cell
then reacts to it:

```js
//| input: palette
const name = qmd.value("palette");          // "viridis" | "magma" | "cividis"
return Plot.plot({ color: { scheme: name }, marks: [/* … */] });
```

## Scrollytelling: `::: {.scrolly}`

A `.scrolly` container pins a visual stage while a column of `.step` scenes scrolls
past it. As each step crosses the middle of the viewport it becomes active: its
`state=` value is written to `data-scrolly-state` on the container (for pure-CSS
effects) and, when the container has a `name=`, into a reactive input of that name.
So a sticky `{js}` cell reacts with `//| input:`, the same graph as everything else.
Scrollytelling, then, is a reactive input driven by scroll position instead of a
slider.

```markdown
::: {.scrolly name="scene"}
[ a {js} cell with //| input: scene that draws the stage ]
::: {.step state="trend"}
First, the steady linear trend.
:::
::: {.step state="spike"}
Now the quadratic spike.
:::
:::
```

The stage is every non-`.step` block in the container; the narration is the `.step`
divs. The first step is active on load, and the input's initial value matches it, so
the cell has a sane value before any scroll. Omit `name=` for pure-CSS scenes.

## Narrated code walkthroughs: `::: {.code-walkthrough}`

A `.code-walkthrough` pins one code block as a sticky panel and scrolls `.step`
prose beside it. Each step's `lines=` focuses a line range in the panel, washing it in
the accent, so the reader's eye tracks the narration through the code.

```markdown
::: {.code-walkthrough}
[ one code block: the sticky panel ]
::: {.step lines="1"}
First we import NumPy.
:::
::: {.step lines="3-5"}
Then one EM iteration: an E-step, then an M-step.
:::
:::
```

The line spec is a comma-separated list of single lines and ranges, so one step can
focus several non-contiguous regions at once: a leading block plus a later call site,
say.

```markdown
::: {.step lines="1-5,8"}
The setup (lines 1 through 5) plus the one call site on line 8.
:::
```

Here `"1-5,8"` lights lines 1 through 5 and line 8 together and dims everything else.
Line numbers are 1-based and count the panel's own lines. Other valid specs are a
single line (`"3"`), a single range (`"3-5"`), or a longer mix (`"1,4,6-8"`). To show
the whole block with nothing dimmed, use `"all"` or leave `lines` off entirely; that is
the right choice for an opening "here is the finished code" step before you start
walking through it.

## Tabbed panels: `::: {.panel-tabset}`

A `.panel-tabset` turns its headings into tabs. Each heading at the shallowest level
present becomes a tab button (emitted as an ARIA `role="tab"`, not a real heading,
so it never pollutes the table of contents); the blocks under it become that tab's
panel. Here is one live:

::: {.panel-tabset}

## Python

Content for the Python tab.

## R

Content for the R tab.

:::

The first tab is selected on load. Readers switch with a click or the keyboard
(arrow keys move between tabs, Home and End jump to the first and last). Panel
content keeps its own ids and source positions, so click-to-source still works
inside a tab, and a `@fig-` reference resolves through it.
